div.ab:
Those things that are seemly are not duly performed, and lo, the world is taken up and busied with vanities. The departed is defrauded of the oblation that benefits him; and they set themselves to call to him in the grave, while he hears not. Things needful are not duly performed; and wrongfully those things that are not fitting are done. Abel offered the firstlings of his flocks with the fatlings thereof;
and righteous Noah sacrificed the clean animals of
those that were left. Abraham gave three victims of three years in the sacrifice which he offered up; and Jacob tithed all that he possessed to God. Virtuous Lot for justice' sake delivered up his daughters; and Moses the great commanded that all the firstlings should be sacrificed. For the tabernacle of witness the Hebrews took gold and silver and fine twisted linen and goodly garments, each man after his ability. With sacrifices and vows and tithes and goodly gifts the just men presented their offerings to God: and thou,—to-day it has fallen to thee to offer the eucharistic bread (perîstâ), and thou hast set at naught and refrained from the offering that benefits thee. Love is grown lukewarm, for men's faith is waxed light, and the soul is darkened from understanding by the increase of iniquity. The eucharistic loaf (qesâthâ), which is the sacrifice of the soul, is withheld, and no man brings it as he ought to the house of atonement. The seemly custom has been left by men, and they are diligent only in the service of evil. They are anxious concerning the inheritance of the dead man, to divide it;
but his memorial they are not accustomed to offer: neither of that which was his nor of their own do they make for him a memorial. Evil heirs, they have taken his possessions, and have forgotten the love of him. As bandits they have divided the goodly garments that he had; and they have cast a lot upon his desirable effects. All bandits peaceably divide the spoil they have carried off: but the heirs with rage and wrangling and strifes, with lawsuits daily in the courts, and with hatred and anger. The heirs then are worse than the bandits; and every way they daily outrage the dead man's love. If there were any love of the Lord in their minds, when they divide the estate of a brother they would make him also an heir; and they would keep for him a portion of his possessions when they are divided, that it might be for his memorial and his oblation after him. But because they are far from justice and duty, they have divided and taken, and have dealt ungratefully and forgotten those things that are seemly. Whoso has the love of God in his soul shews his love after the dead is gone, and forgets him not; and with memorials and oblations and prayers he brings in his cause before God to the holy temple.
